#title()

I will make a strong claim here in this essay, that modern world makes
alienated from _others_, from _ourselves_, and from the _world around us_. This
is a strong claim in which I will back up with my experience. Of course, I
didn't come up with the idea, I'm just writing about my experience with it.

First, I'll need to define what being "alienated" means. Alienated is a marxist
term, but I will define it for my own purposes. It's used to describe a
specific _feeling_ of being "separated" from the
world around us. Abstracted. Divorced.

The feeling of NOT being alienated is to feel connected. Connected to our work,
connected to other people, and connected to the world. To feel joy in our work
and our play. To feel the warmth on the inside when we spend time with our
loved ones. And to _feel_ the feelings of being at peace and joyful in our
environment, whether it's through music or in nature.

I belive, my thesis, is that the modern environment, whether it is capitalism
or not, _encourages_ us to be _alienated_ from others. This is not our fault
but the fault of our environment! And that, to find true happiness, the most we
can do is _to ourselves_ wake up to the reality, and once woken up _try_ to
wake up others (but not at the expense of ourselves).

In my writing, I'd rather speak to _my_ experience rather than be philosophical
and haughty in trying to say _my_ experience applies to all experiences. But,
to quote Ralph Elliot:

#quote(attribution: [Ralph Elliot], block: true)[
  Who knows but that, on the lower frequencies, I speak for you?
]


